/*

You are working as a data analyst for a high-security research facility. Recently, an internal security breach occurred — classified information was leaked from somewhere inside the building.

Investigators have discovered a key clue:

The person responsible accessed two different security zones, one right after the other, and the time gap between those two accesses is crucial for reconstructing the event timeline.

Your job is to analyze badge-swipe logs stored in a table called access_logs.

The table has the following structure:

id (int): Primary key
employee_id (int): The ID of the employee who swiped their badge
zone (varchar): The security zone they entered
access_time (timestamp): The exact moment the badge was used to enter the zone
Each row represents a single door swipe made by an employee.

Your Task

Write a SQL query that determines, for each employee, the time difference (in seconds) between their two most recent badge swipes into two different security zones.

Only employees who have accessed at least two different zones should appear in the result.

Output Columns:

employee_id – The employee’s ID
seconds_diff – The absolute time difference (in seconds) between the timestamps of their two most recent different-zone accesses
Results must be ordered first by seconds_diff in ascending order, and then by employee_id in ascending order when multiple employees have the same time difference.

Notes:

Each entry in access_logs represents a single badge swipe; duplicates do not occur in this system.
Only different zones count — two most recent accesses to the same zone must not be paired.
An employee must have accessed at least two different zones (two or more), not exactly two — if they accessed three, four, or more zones, the two most recent distinct zones should still be used.
For each zone, only the most recent access time is relevant.
The time difference must always be returned as a non-negative number of seconds.
Each employee must appear only once in the output.
The underlying table is partitioned by day, and each query operates on a single day’s partition, so you do not need to filter by date or apply any additional time-range conditions.
It is guaranteed by the facility’s access control system that no employee can swipe into two different zones at the exact same timestamp, so identical access_time values for different zones will never occur.
For the following sample data:

| employee_id |     zone     |      access_time        |
+-------------+--------------+-------------------------+
|     1       |   Alpha      | 2023-06-01 12:00:00     |
|     1       |   Beta       | 2023-06-01 12:03:00     |
|     1       |   Alpha      | 2023-06-01 12:04:00     |
|     2       |   Gamma      | 2023-06-01 09:10:00     |
|     2       |   Gamma      | 2023-06-01 09:12:00     |
|     2       |   Delta      | 2023-06-01 09:15:00     |
|     3       |   Omega      | 2023-06-01 14:00:00     |
Expected output is:

| employee_id | seconds_diff |
+-------------+--------------+
|      1      |      60      |
|      2      |     180      |
GLHF!



*/

WITH zone_time AS (
    SELECT employee_id,
           zone,
           access_time,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY employee_id, zone ORDER BY access_time DESC) AS zone_rn
    FROM access_logs
),
ranked AS (
    SELECT employee_id,
           access_time,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY employee_id ORDER BY access_time DESC) AS rn
    FROM zone_time
    WHERE zone_rn = 1
)
SELECT employee_id,
       EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (MAX(access_time) - MIN(access_time))) AS seconds_diff
FROM ranked
WHERE rn <= 2
GROUP BY employee_id
HAVING COUNT(*) = 2
ORDER BY seconds_diff, employee_id;

